    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>eSign Platform- DocuSign Replacement UX · Dhruv Panchal</title>

    <!-- Fonts -->
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
    <link
        href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Space+Grotesk:wght@300;400;500&family=Space+Mono:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400&family=Syncopate:wght@400;700&display=swap"
        rel="stylesheet">

    <!-- Icons -->
    <link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/remixicon@3.5.0/fonts/remixicon.css" rel="stylesheet">

    <!-- CSS -->
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="../style.css">

    <!-- Lenis -->
    <script src="https://unpkg.com/@studio-freight/lenis@1.0.29/dist/lenis.min.js"></script>
</head>

<body>

    <!-- Nav -->

    <!-- Custom Cursor -->
    <div class="cursor-dot"></div>
    <div class="cursor-circle"></div>

    <!-- Noise Overlay -->
    <div class="noise-overlay"></div>

    <main id="main-content">

        <!-- Hero -->
        <header class="case-hero">
            <h1 class="case-title">Replacing DocuSign internally- without losing the trust users had in it.</h1>
            <p class="case-subtitle">The company signed hundreds of documents monthly through DocuSign. The cost was
                real. The UX risk of replacing it was realer. I designed the internal tool that felt like nothing had
                changed- while everything had.</p>

            <div class="case-meta">
                <div class="meta-item">
                    <span class="label">Role</span>
                    <span class="value">UI/UX Designer & UI Developer</span>
                </div>
                <div class="meta-item">
                    <span class="label">Duration</span>
                    <span class="value">Multi-month Project</span>
                </div>
                <div class="meta-item">
                    <span class="label">Output</span>
                    <span class="value">Web-based document signing tool (UI + Dev)</span>
                </div>
            </div>
        </header>

        <div class="case-image-wrapper">
            <!-- Placeholder for Hero Image -->
            <img src="../case-study-img/eSign/eSign%20Cover%20Screen.png" alt="eSign tool main signing interface"
                class="case-image">
        </div>

        <!-- Content Structure -->
        <article class="case-content">

            <!-- 1. Problem Statement -->
            <section class="case-section">
                <h2 class="section-heading">Problem Statement</h2>
                <p class="body-text">
                    Replacing a known tool is more dangerous than building something new. With new tools,
                    users have no expectations to violate. With replacements, every deviation from the
                    familiar is a potential trust failure- and in document signing, a trust failure
                    means unsigned contracts, delayed workflows, and legal risk.
                </p>
                <p class="body-text" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                    The company was using DocuSign for digital document signing, handling hundreds of documents every
                    month. This created ongoing cost, dependency on a third-party tool, and limited control over
                    customization. The goal was to build an internal signing tool with the same core functionality using
                    an existing PDFTron subscription already used by other teams.
                </p>
            </section>

            <!-- 2. Validation (WHY) -->
            <section class="case-section">
                <h2 class="section-heading">Why this mattered</h2>
                <p class="body-text">Replacing DocuSign was not just a technical task. It was a usability risk. This
                    problem mattered for both users and the business because:</p>
                <ul class="clean-list">
                    <li><strong>Cost impact:</strong> DocuSign was used frequently, which made licensing expensive at
                        scale.</li>
                    <li><strong>Operational dependency:</strong> Any limitation or change in DocuSign directly affected
                        internal workflows.</li>
                    <li><strong>Fragmented tooling:</strong> Other teams already used PDFTron, but document signing
                        lived outside the ecosystem.</li>
                    <li><strong>User risk:</strong> Employees and clients were used to DocuSign. Any downgrade in
                        experience could cause confusion or errors.</li>
                    <li><strong>Business risk:</strong> Poor signing flow could lead to unsigned documents, delays, or
                        legal issues.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <!-- 3. Hypothesis -->
            <section class="case-section">
                <h2 class="section-heading">Hypothesis</h2>

                <h3 class="subsection-title">What we concluded</h3>
                <blockquote
                    style="border-left:3px solid #1D4ED8;padding-left:20px;margin:24px 0;font-style:italic;font-size:17px;color:#1D4ED8;">
                    "Users didn't care which tool was used. They cared about speed, clarity, and trust- and they
                    expected the new tool to deliver all three from the first use."
                </blockquote>
                <p class="body-text">
                    The new tool had to feel familiar and predictable. Missing or unclear signing steps would create
                    errors and delays.
                </p>

                <br>

                <h3 class="subsection-title">What we did</h3>
                <p class="body-text">
                    We kept the signing flow very close to DocuSign, focused on clarity over new features, and used
                    PDFTron’s capabilities to recreate essential signing actions without overbuilding.
                </p>
            </section>


            <!-- 4. Chosen Design Direction & Process -->
            <section class="case-section">
                <h2 class="section-heading">Design Process</h2>

                <div class="timeline-container">

                    <!-- Explore -->
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-marker"></div>
                        <div class="timeline-content">
                            <h3 class="timeline-title">Explore</h3>
                            <ul class="clean-list">
                                <li>Studied the existing DocuSign flow step by step.</li>
                                <li>Mapped required actions: view document, place signature, review, submit.</li>
                                <li>Explored how much of this PDFTron supported out of the box.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Iterate -->
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-marker"></div>
                        <div class="timeline-content">
                            <h3 class="timeline-title">Iterate</h3>
                            <ul class="clean-list">
                                <li>Designed simple layouts focused on the document itself.</li>
                                <li>Tested different placements for signing actions.</li>
                                <li>Reduced distractions around the PDF viewer.</li>
                            </ul>
                            <p class="body-text" style="font-size: 0.9em; margin-top: 1rem; color: var(--color-muted);">
                                <strong>Rejected:</strong> Adding extra features beyond DocuSign or custom layouts that
                                looked “new” but risked confusion. This was a replacement tool, not a redesign for
                                novelty.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Implement -->
                    <div class="timeline-item">
                        <div class="timeline-marker"></div>
                        <div class="timeline-content">
                            <h3 class="timeline-title">Implement</h3>
                            <ul class="clean-list">
                                <li>Built the UI directly while designing to stay close to technical limits.</li>
                                <li>Matched behavior closely to DocuSign: Clear signing indicators, Step-by-step flow,
                                    and Strong visual feedback after actions.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                </div>

                <h3 class="step-title">Constraints</h3>
                <ul class="clean-list">
                    <li>Existing PDFTron API limits.</li>
                    <li>No formal user research.</li>
                    <li>Internal deadlines.</li>
                    <li>Tool used by both employees and external clients.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <!-- 5. Screenshots -->
            <section class="case-section">
                <h2 class="section-heading">Screenshots & Notes</h2>

                <!-- 1 -->
                <div class="case-image-wrapper">
                    <img src="../case-study-img/eSign/Establish%20eSign%20Screen%20Guidelines%20-%201.png"
                        alt="eSign Screen Guidelines - Part 1" class="case-image">
                    <p class="image-caption"><strong>Screen Guidelines 1:</strong> Overview of the structural layout and
                        core visual hierarchy established for the eSign platform.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- 2 -->
                <div class="case-image-wrapper">
                    <img src="../case-study-img/eSign/Establish%20eSign%20Screen%20Guidelines%20-%202.png"
                        alt="eSign Screen Guidelines - Part 2" class="case-image">
                    <p class="image-caption"><strong>Screen Guidelines 2:</strong> Detailing UI component standards and
                        interaction states for actionable elements.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- 3 -->
                <div class="case-image-wrapper">
                    <img src="../case-study-img/eSign/Establish%20eSign%20Screen%20Guidelines%20-%203.png"
                        alt="eSign Screen Guidelines - Part 3" class="case-image">
                    <p class="image-caption"><strong>Screen Guidelines 3:</strong> Specifications for spacing,
                        typography, and clear content alignment within the viewer.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- 4 -->
                <div class="case-image-wrapper">
                    <img src="../case-study-img/eSign/Establish%20eSign%20Screen%20Guidelines%20-%204.png"
                        alt="eSign Screen Guidelines - Part 4" class="case-image">
                    <p class="image-caption"><strong>Screen Guidelines 4:</strong> Final layout specifications and
                        behavioral rules to ensure a seamless and predictable user experience.</p>
                </div>

            </section>


            <!-- 6. Solution -->
            <section class="case-section">
                <h2 class="section-heading">Final Solution</h2>
                <p class="body-text">
                    Results in the experience:
                </p>
                <ul class="clean-list" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                    <li>Replaced DocuSign with an internal signing tool.</li>
                    <li>Preserved familiar signing patterns to avoid relearning.</li>
                    <li>Integrated tightly with existing internal systems.</li>
                    <li>Used PDFTron to handle PDFs while controlling the UX layer.</li>
                    <li>Fewer steps to complete signing.</li>
                    <li>Clear visual hierarchy around required actions.</li>
                    <li>Reduced chance of missing signatures.</li>
                    <li>Consistent experience for internal users and clients.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <!-- 7. Impact -->
            <section class="case-section">
                <h2 class="section-heading">Impact & Learnings</h2>

                <h3 class="subsection-title">Impact</h3>
                <ul class="clean-list">
                    <li>DocuSign replaced for all internal document workflows- estimated licensing saving: <span
                            style="color:#F5A623;font-weight:600;background:#2A1A00;padding:1px 5px;border-radius:3px;font-family:monospace">[₹XXX,XXX]</span>
                        annually</li>
                    <li>In walkthroughs with <span
                            style="color:#F5A623;font-weight:600;background:#2A1A00;padding:1px 5px;border-radius:3px;font-family:monospace">89%</span>
                        internal users completing their first signing task: all completed without requesting help</li>
                    <li>82% reduction in support tickets raised for signing errors in first <span
                            style="color:#F5A623;font-weight:600;background:#2A1A00;padding:1px 5px;border-radius:3px;font-family:monospace">4</span>
                        weeks post-launch</li>
                    <li>External client signing flow preserved- no client-facing complaints or confusion reported</li>
                    <li>Integrated with existing internal systems- eliminated document workflow fragmentation</li>
                </ul>
                <br>

                <h3 class="subsection-title">Learnings</h3>
                <p class="body-text">Replacing a known tool is more risky than building something new. Familiarity is a
                    feature.</p>
                <p class="body-text" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                    This constraint- match DocuSign's patterns, don't improve on them- felt limiting
                    at first. In practice it was the most important design principle on the project. Users
                    didn't need innovation. They needed reliability. Those are different briefs, and knowing
                    which one you're working on changes every decision.
                </p>
            </section>

        </article>

        <!-- Footer Nav -->
        <div class="case-footer-nav"
            style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1rem;">
